id: quiz.rust.shared-state
kind: quiz
track: rust
title: Shared state in async Rust
question: How do you share mutable state between Tokio tasks, and when would you avoid it?
expects:
  - arc
  - mutex
  - channels
  - ownership
  - contention
misuse:
  - arc mutex everywhere => hides the ownership boundary and serializes unrelated work
  - tokio mutex by default => pays async overhead for guards that never await
related:
  - rust.arc
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - design.shared-state
follow_ups:
  - what happens if you hold a std MutexGuard across an await?
  - how would you avoid contention on the hot path?
answer: |
  Prefer ownership transfer: move the state to one task and use channels. When
  state is genuinely shared, Arc<Mutex<T>> (or Arc<RwLock<_>> for read-mostly)
  with a small critical section; the guard releases on drop. Reach for
  tokio::sync::Mutex only when the guard must span an await. Atomics for single
  values. If AppState keeps growing locks, that is the signal to restructure.
